      <p>What the numbers show:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>French and CEC rounds dominated.</strong> Together they account for 118,250 invitations, about 79% of the 2026 total. French rounds had the lowest cutoffs of any large round type, mostly in the 380s to 430s.</li>
        <li><strong>CEC cutoffs crept up.</strong> In the first quarter CEC cutoffs ran from 507 to 511. From June onward every CEC round cut off between 516 and 523, while round sizes shrank from 6,000 to 8,000 in January and February to 1,000 to 3,000 in most later rounds.</li>
        <li><strong>PNP rounds were frequent but small.</strong> There were 21 PNP rounds, usually a few hundred invitations each. Cutoffs of 697 to 805 reflect the 600 points a nomination adds.</li>
        <li><strong>Some categories had no rounds.</strong> As of November 13, 2026, there has been no STEM, education or researcher round in 2026, even though all three are current categories.</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly volume varied.</strong> March was the busiest month (20,982 invitations) and May the quietest (8,214). October had 17,694 before the pause.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="cutoffs" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How CRS Cutoffs and Tie-Breaking Work</h2>

      <p>
        The CRS cutoff is the score of the lowest-ranked candidate invited in a round. IRCC first decides the round type and how many invitations to issue, then invites eligible candidates from the top score down until it reaches that number. The cutoff is a result of the pool and the round size, not a pass mark set in advance. That is why cutoffs differ so much between round types: a French round only competes among French-proficient candidates, while a PNP round only includes nominees.
      </p>
      <p>
        When several candidates share the cutoff score, IRCC applies the tie-breaking rule: candidates with that score who submitted their profile before the stated date and time are invited. In round 453, for example, candidates with 477 who entered the pool before June 4, 2026 at 21:48:22 UTC were invited.
      </p>
      <p>
        To see where you might sit, compare your score with recent cutoffs for the round types you are eligible for, not just the overall latest draw. If you are a B.C. worker weighing a provincial route, our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-crs-calculator" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP vs CRS calculator comparison</a> explains how the two scores differ, and the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP Calculator</a> estimates your provincial registration score.
      </p>

      <h2 id="november" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The November 3, 2026 CRS Changes</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC's October 16, 2026 release, "Canada refocusing Express Entry on top talent that drives economic growth," and its CRS criteria page (updated November 3, 2026) set out the new rules. The main points for anyone reading draw results:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Labour Market Integration factor.</strong> A new factor worth up to 150 points scores either Canadian work experience or a qualifying job offer, not both. Canadian experience earns up to 150 points without a spouse or partner (80 for length of work, 70 for high-wage work) and up to 140 with one (70 plus 70).</li>
        <li><strong>High-wage job offers.</strong> A qualifying offer earns 100 points if the occupation's median wage is at least twice the overall average median wage, 70 points at 1.5 to under 2 times, and 50 points at 1.3 to under 1.5 times. Offers below 1.3 times earn no points. The test uses the occupation's NOC code, not your own salary.</li>
        <li><strong>No LMIA needed.</strong> A job offer no longer needs a Labour Market Impact Assessment to qualify, and an LMIA no longer adds points. IRCC says officers still check that offers are genuine.</li>
        <li><strong>Additional points.</strong> A provincial or territorial nomination is still worth 600 points. The old arranged employment item no longer appears in the additional points table.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because the grid changed, 2026 cutoffs before November 3 and cutoffs after it measure different things. Treat the tables above as history, not a target.
      </p>

      <h2 id="pnp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Express Entry Draws and the BC PNP</h2>

      <p>
        For B.C. applicants, the PNP rounds matter most. If you get a nomination through the BC PNP's Express Entry BC option, you receive 600 extra CRS points and, with cutoffs in the 697 to 805 range so far in 2026, nominees have generally been invited in a following PNP round, though IRCC guarantees no timing and your profile must stay valid. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry BC guide</a> explains how to link the two systems. The BC PNP holds its own draws, separate from IRCC's; you can follow those on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP draws tracker</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="after-ita" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to Do After an Express Entry Invitation</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Check the deadline.</strong> An invitation is valid for 60 days, starting the day after it is issued. Submit a complete application for permanent residence in that window.</li>
        <li><strong>Recalculate your score.</strong> If your situation, or your spouse's or partner's, has changed since you entered the pool, recalculate. IRCC says that if your new score is below the cutoff of your round, you should decline the invitation.</li>
        <li><strong>Gather documents early.</strong> Expect to need police certificates, an immigration medical exam, proof of funds if your program requires it, and evidence for every claim in your profile, such as reference letters for work experience.</li>
        <li><strong>Decline if you can't apply.</strong> Declining returns your profile to the pool and doesn't affect whether you are invited again later. If you neither apply nor decline, the invitation expires and IRCC removes your profile.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't create a second profile</strong> to "reset" your position; keep one accurate profile and update it when facts change.</li>
      </ol>

